[test-suite name=process ring=1
  [doc [#
conformance fixtures for cx-stdlib/process (spec/std-lib/process.md §6).

   Each program imports the bundled module via [?lib 'cx-stdlib/process']
   (resolver MUST be quoted) then calls [$process:fn args]. The LAST
   expression is the rendered result.

   DETERMINISM / EFFECTFUL MODULE: cx-stdlib/process is impure Tier-B
   runtime (§1). EVERY public function is `impure` (§3) and operates on a
   spawned child process, so EVERY one requires the `subprocess`
   capability — both the spawn-family entry points AND the operations on
   an already-spawned child (§7: "Every function in this module operates
   on a spawned child process and therefore requires the `subprocess`
   capability"). The stdlib eval runner evaluates each case under
   `code.new_env()`, which grants NO capabilities (deny-by-default per
   spec/core/security.md §2). At the effect point the active set is
   checked FIRST; with the `subprocess` grant absent the function raises
   E_CAP_DENIED = `cx-err:CXER0271` (security.md §4: "a denied effect at
   the effect point raises CXER0271"; process.md §7: effectful functions
   "raise cx-err:CXER0271 when the grant is absent").

   Consequently the deterministic, runner-faithful, spec-grounded
   assertion for the happy-path call of every process function is the
   capability-denied error CXER0271 — real subprocess side effects
   (spawning binaries, signalling, allocating ptys) are neither
   reproducible nor safe inside the conformance harness, and AUTHORING.md
   ("Capability-gated / effectful") directs us to fixture the
   capability-denied error rather than real side effects. Each public
   function therefore gets a CXER0271 case exercising its real call shape
   (correct arity / arg kinds per the §3 [?def] signature). Several
   functions additionally get distinct call-shape cases for spec-called-out
   behaviors (argv-injection literal, empty-argv, env augment/clear, cwd,
   timeout, $check, pipeline stages, signal atoms) — all still gated to
   CXER0271 under the runner's empty capability set.

   PROCESS-SPECIFIC ERROR CODES (CXER4000–CXER4013, §5): these are
   documented behaviors that fire only AFTER the capability check passes
   (a granted spawn that then hits empty-argv, missing-cwd, not-found,
   timeout, pipefail+check, closed-handle, non-group-leader, pty-unsupported,
   encoding-invalid, etc.). Under the runner's empty capability set the
   capability check short-circuits FIRST, so a correct implementation
   raises CXER0271 — NOT the CXER4xxx code — for a program with no
   `grant`. Asserting CXER4001/4003/4006/4007/4009/4011/4012 on a
   NO-GRANT case would therefore FAIL a correct implementation (a wrong
   fixture is worse than a missing one, AUTHORING.md "Grounding").

   A DENY-ONLY SUITE HIDES DEFECTS — the #1028 lesson, recorded here
   because this header is what licensed the gap. Every case below
   028 is a no-grant CXER0271 case, and several were written to
   exercise a specific CALL SHAPE (env augment/clear, cwd, stage
   `[opts …]`). A deny case cannot do that: the guard raises before the
   body runs, so the shape it names is never reached. process-019
   asserted a pipeline stage `[opts cwd="/tmp"]` for months while
   `proc_pipeline` dropped `[opts …]` on the floor entirely — the
   fixture was green throughout, because CXER0271 is all it ever
   tested. That is the vacuous-gate shape: a fixture whose subject is
   unreachable is coverage of the capability guard, not of the feature
   in the program text.

   So the rule for this suite is now: a deny case pins the CXER0271
   contract and NOTHING else, and any option or element whose behavior
   is worth asserting gets a `grant='subprocess'` TWIN that actually
   runs it (028-064). The deny cases stay — process-019 included — as
   the capability-contract half of each pair.

   THE ENCODING CASES (060-064), §4.7. `$encoding` was declared on all
   three entry points and read by NONE until #1028 wired `pipeline`;
   060-064 close the other two (#1035). 060 pins `:bytes` on `run` —
   the `::bytes` type ascription on the captured attributes is the
   observable difference, since the byte count is identical either way.
   061 and 063 pin the accepted-set refusal (CXER0100) at `run` and
   `spawn`: a misspelled encoding must not silently mean the default,
   because the default is a DECODE. 062 pins CXER4008 on an invalid
   UTF-8 sequence under the default encoding. 064 is the other half of
   063 — a VALID `$encoding` on `spawn` is accepted and the child
   spawns — because a refusal case alone cannot show that the new
   validation admits what it should.

   THE TWO RESOURCE CASES (058, 059), §4.4. Both assert that `run` has
   STOPPED OWNING the child by the time it returns, and neither can be
   written as a deny case for the reason above.

   058 — a child inherits the parent's non-CLOEXEC descriptors, so
   `ls /dev/fd` INSIDE the child counts the PARENT's open fds. §4.4
   requires `run` to close the parent ends of the pipes it opened, so
   that count must be FLAT across captured runs. Pre-#1034 nothing
   closed a parent-side capture fd anywhere in the module — `wait` is a
   bare `waitpid` and `fd_slurp` reads to EOF and returns the bytes — so
   this read `false` and the raw counts climbed 5, 7, 9, 11, two per
   call, for the life of the interpreter.

   059 — §4.5's FORCED phase, reached only by a child that ignores
   SIGTERM, plus §2.3's "when `signaled` is true, `exit-code` carries the
   platform's signal-encoded status (128 + signum)": 128 + SIGKILL(9) =
   137. The exit code is what makes the reap observable. Pre-#1034 the
   forced kill went through V's `signal_kill()`, whose last act is
   `status = .aborted`, and V's `wait()` returns without a syscall for
   any status outside running/stopped — so the killer disqualified its
   own victim from ever being waited on. The child stayed a zombie and
   `exit-code` kept the stale 0 from the last liveness poll: this case
   read `exit-code=0` for a process killed by a signal. A child that
   DIES on SIGTERM never showed any of it, because the grace loop polls
   `is_alive()` and V implements that as a `waitpid(WNOHANG)` that reaps
   as a side effect — which is why every well-behaved fixture was green.
   Bounded: a ~100 ms budget plus §4.5's fixed grace.

   NOTE: no inter-fixture comment blocks appear between cases (a comment
   between sections risks capture into the preceding section payload);
   all explanatory prose lives in this doc header or in tags.
#]]

 [case id=process-001-run-cap-denied level=core
  [tags process run capability CXER0271]
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-stdlib/process']
[$process:run ("echo", "hello")]
#]]
  [out-err [#
cx-err:CXER0271
#]]
 ]

